Unauthenticated slow body can starve background work
Link: https://chatgpt.com/codex/cloud/security/findings/9bad4d482060819180bd2fc7cdec6e32?sev=&repo=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fjustincormack%2Fargmin&sort=date_desc
Criticality: medium (attack path: medium)
Status: fixed

# Resolution
Fixed in commit [`0ab1ebc25ed62297541417691e6a35635de5dace`](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/0ab1ebc25ed62297541417691e6a35635de5dace) (`Bound unauthenticated HTTP request pressure`).

The finding was valid. The fix adds two independent availability controls:

- Pre-authentication request-body reads now have a 60-second absolute deadline in addition to the existing per-frame idle timeout. The bound covers complete buffered request bodies and multipart POST fields read before authentication establishes the streaming context; authenticated object streaming remains governed by route validity and idle timeouts. Frame reads use a timer-first biased `select!` and recheck the absolute deadline before returning a successful frame, so a queued frame cannot win an expiry race or move multipart processing beyond the pre-authentication boundary.
- Foreground maintenance pressure is now capacity-relative rather than true for every nonzero request count. Each HTTP server registers its admission capacity process-wide, and maintenance suppression starts only at the 75% admission high-water mark. One slow request therefore cannot suppress backfill candidate scans, routine backfill, opportunistic scans, or routine metadata checkpoints while request capacity remains available.

The admitted request permit and inflight metric guard are also represented by one `HttpRequestAdmission` owner and transferred into the response body. Response conversion no longer creates a second guard, avoiding transient false pressure at the quantitative threshold. The threat model and multihost plan now document the absolute pre-authentication deadline and reserved maintenance capacity.

Regression coverage verifies steady slow chunks hit the absolute deadline, expiry wins over an already-ready frame without consuming it, response handoff does not double-count inflight work, one request out of 32 does not signal foreground pressure, and 24 out of 32 does. Verification completed with 3,324 affected tests, the full 7,833-test workspace suite, and `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings` all passing.

