# Summary
Introduced. The newly added inflight_requests guard in server-http starts before body collection/authentication, and server-core uses that metric as the sole active foreground-pressure signal for denying several background work classes.
After acquiring the request semaphore, the HTTP handler now immediately creates an inflight_requests guard. This happens before routing completes for normal buffered requests, before the full request body is collected, and before the S3 request is authenticated/authorized by the frontend. The body collection helper uses only a per-frame idle timeout that resets on every received chunk, so a client can send a valid non-streaming request with a large Content-Length or chunked body and drip small chunks just below the idle timeout. While that unauthenticated request is being collected, MetricsSnapshot.inflight_requests remains greater than zero. BackgroundWorkAdmission then reports foreground pressure whenever inflight_requests is nonzero and denies BackfillCandidateScan, RoutineBackfill, OpportunisticScan, and RoutineMetadataCheckpoint. This lets a low-bandwidth unauthenticated network client suppress important maintenance/backfill/checkpoint work without filling all request slots.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the HTTP inflight request guard starts before buffered body collection and before S3 authentication/authorization.
- [x] Confirm buffered non-streaming requests collect the complete body before constructing/handling the authenticated S3Request.
- [x] Dynamically confirm the body idle timeout is per-frame and resets for slow steady chunks.
- [x] Confirm server-core treats `inflight_requests > 0` as active foreground pressure and uses it to deny BackfillCandidateScan, RoutineBackfill, OpportunisticScan, and RoutineMetadataCheckpoint.
- [x] Confirm the denial can be triggered by a single inflight request metric, i.e. without exhausting all request semaphore slots.
## Report
Validated the finding as a logic/availability issue, not a memory-safety crash. Crash reproduction is not expected for this bug class; the direct PoC tests complete successfully while demonstrating the vulnerable state. Valgrind was unavailable in the container, and an ASan attempt failed because stable Rust rejected `-Z sanitizer=address`. I then used LLDB against the debug test binary and stopped in the real admission path.

Key code evidence: in `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:898-948`, the handler acquires the request semaphore and immediately creates `observability::inflight_requests_guard()` before routing/body buffering completes. For the non-streaming buffered path, `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:1164-1193` calls `collect_body_with_limit(...).await` before constructing `S3Request`; authentication is later inside `HttpFrontend::handle_service_request`, where routing/authentication occurs at `crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:1006-1113`. The collector wraps each individual `limited.frame()` in `tokio::time::timeout` at `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:5267-5282`, so the timeout resets for every chunk. In server-core, suppressible background classes return `DeniedForegroundPressure` when `pressure.foreground` is true at `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/runtime.rs:274-303`; active foreground pressure is solely `snapshot.inflight_requests > 0` at `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/runtime.rs:409-411`.

Dynamic PoCs added as a temporary patch: `cargo test -p server-http collect_body_idle_timeout_resets_for_slow_steady_chunks -- --nocapture` passed. This starts a local Hyper HTTP/1 service that calls `collect_body_with_limit` directly, then sends chunked body bytes every 70ms under a 120ms idle timeout; total upload time exceeds one idle timeout but returns `ok:abc`, proving slow steady unauthenticated body collection can persist. `cargo test -p server-core foreground_inflight_request_guard_denies_suppressible_background_classes -- --nocapture` passed and exercises the actual `observability::inflight_requests_guard()` plus `BackgroundWorkAdmission::try_acquire` path, proving one inflight request denies BackfillCandidateScan, RoutineBackfill, OpportunisticScan, and RoutineMetadataCheckpoint while non-suppressed classes remain admissible.

Debugger evidence from `lldb-policy-trace.log`: LLDB stopped at `BackgroundWorkAdmission::policy_denial_event_for_pressure` with `class=BackfillCandidateScan` and `pressure=(foreground = true, durable_backlog = false)`. The backtrace shows the path `policy_denial_event_for_pressure` <- `policy_denial_event` <- `foreground_inflight_request_guard_denies_suppressible_background_classes`, confirming the denial is caused by foreground pressure derived from the inflight request metric, not durable backlog or limits.

Relevant command outputs: server-core PoC: `running 1 test ... foreground_inflight_request_guard_denies_suppressible_background_classes ... ok`. server-http PoC: `running 1 test ... collect_body_idle_timeout_resets_for_slow_steady_chunks ... ok`. ASan attempt: `error: the option Z is only accepted on the nightly compiler`. Valgrind attempt: `valgrind not installed in container`.

